In this lesson we'll look at function notation, which is really just the language we use to talk about functions. First up, we name functions with letters, and we write f of x to mean the value of f at x. So if f of x equals three x minus five, then f of two is just three times two minus five, which gives us one. That whole process is called evaluating, and it just means substituting a number, or even another expression, in for x. For instance, f of a is three a minus five, and f of x plus one is three x plus one minus five, which simplifies to three x minus two. Next, the formal notation spells out three things together: the name of the function, the domain, which is the set of allowed inputs, and the co-domain, which is the set the outputs come from, along with the rule that tells you how to turn each input into an output. Sometimes you'll also see it written with a maps to arrow, where x maps to x squared, for example. Now let's see it in action.